meta {
  name: Create Parent and Variant Products (Flow 2)
  type: http
  seq: 15
}

post {
  url: {{pim_base_url_v3}}/products
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Authorization: Bearer {{access_token}}
  Content-Type: application/json
  Accept: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "sku": "WC-TEST-PARENT-001",
    "label": "WC Test Parent",
    "product_family_id": "6a38f5d8c3d65a5f868b186e"
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 201
  res.body.data.product_level: eq 0
}

docs {
  Confirmed live 2026-09-08 -- `USER_FLOWS.md` Flow 2. Created then
  deleted in the same test run (parent id was `6aa04a322b6fd055ffb7cae4`,
  variant id `6aa04a3b2b6fd055ffb7cae5`) -- no downstream dependents, so
  both were cleaned up via the confirmed `DELETE` rather than kept.

  This base request captures the **parent** create (standalone,
  `product_level: 0` at this point). The variant was created as a
  second, separate `POST /products` request:
  `{"sku":"WC-TEST-VARIANT-001","product_family_id":
  "6a38f5d8c3d65a5f868b186e","parent_id":"<parent id>"}` -- `201`,
  `product_level: 2` on the variant.

  ## Findings
  - After the variant was created, re-fetching the **parent** showed
    `product_level` flipped from `0` to `1` (a standalone product starts
    at 0; becoming a parent bumps it to 1) and `num_variations`
    incremented `0`→`1`.
  - The family's own `total_products` count
    (`GET /product-families/{family_id}`) updated live from 7 to 9,
    confirming both new products were correctly attributed to the family.
  - `overwritten_attributes` stayed empty on the variant in this test (no
    attribute values were set on either product, so there was nothing to
    overwrite) -- attribute-inheritance-copying behavior itself wasn't
    exercised.
}
